EDIT(1)                        P32W Unix                         EDIT(1)

NAME
       edit - a text file in a window, changed with the keys and mouse

SYNOPSIS
       edit [file]

DESCRIPTION
       edit shows a file's text and changes it. Under the window
       manager (wm(1)) it has a window of its own, 72 columns by
       22 lines, which its corner makes bigger or smaller; with
       no window manager it takes the whole of the video card's
       screen (video(4)), and the keys alone work it.

       What is typed goes into the text where the cursor is, the
       block that is lit. The top line has the words File and
       Edit, which are menus, and the file's name, with a * after
       it when the text has been changed since it was saved. The
       last line tells the line and the column the cursor is in,
       and what has just been done or could not be. A line longer
       than the window is wide is not folded: the window moves
       sideways over the text to keep the cursor in it.

       A file that is not there yet is made when the text is
       saved. With no file named, a name is asked for then.

       It is P8X's write brought to this machine, where write(1)
       is another command.

THE MOUSE
       on the text         puts the cursor there
       on File or Edit     lets the menu down while the button is
                           held; the item it is let go on is
                           chosen
       on the bar at the right
                           on its upper half, a page back; on its
                           lower half, a page on. The box in it is
                           where in the text the window is

       The File menu has Save, Save as (a name is asked for, on
       the last line) and Close. The Edit menu has Cut line,
       Paste, Find (what to find is asked for) and Find again.
       Each has a key, below.

KEYS
       the arrows     move the cursor
       ^A, ^E         to the beginning of its line, to its end
       ^Y, ^V         a page back, a page on
       Return         a new line
       Backspace      takes away the character before the cursor
       ^D             ... the one under it
       ^K             cut: the cursor's line is taken out and
                      kept. Lines cut one after another are kept
                      together
       ^U             paste: what was cut is put in at the cursor
       ^W             find: the cursor goes to the next place
                      the text asked for is, and from the top
                      again after the end
       ^G             find the same again
       ^O             save
       ^X             close. If the text was changed it asks
                      first: y saves it, n does not, ESC goes on

       Where something is asked for, Backspace takes a character
       back, ^U all of it, Return ends it, and ESC or a press of
       the mouse gives it up.

EXAMPLES
       win edit notes
       edit /usr/examples/hello.c
              from a terminal's shell: the terminal waits until
              it ends
       wm -s /usr/examples/edit.hand
              a hand plays it: text typed, the cursor placed, a
              line cut and pasted, the file saved

       It is on the finder's Apps menu, and its File menu's Edit
       opens the file picked with it (finder(1)).

FILES
       /usr/src/cmd/edit.c        its source

SEE ALSO
       vi(1), ed(1), view(1), finder(1), wm(1), win(1), gfx(3)

DIAGNOSTICS
       What could not be read or written, and why, is said on the
       last line.

BUGS
       No part of the text can be marked with the mouse: what is
       cut is whole lines.
       Nothing that was done can be undone.
       A character that is not printable shows as ? and is kept
       as it was.
       Closing the window by its box ends it without asking, and
       what was not saved is lost.
